Since you're on **.NET 10**, you should use the built-in `TimeProvider` class — no custom wrapper needed.

## How to adopt `TimeProvider`

**1. Inject `TimeProvider` into your class:**

```csharp
namespace NeedsWrappers.Services;

public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        // ... rest of method uses timeProvider.GetUtcNow() instead of DateTime.UtcNow
    }
}
```

**2. Register in DI (Program.cs):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**3. Test with `FakeTimeProvider`:**

Install the test helper:
```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

Then in your test:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 25, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.Contains("20260625_120000", path); // deterministic!
```

You can also advance time with `fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1))`.

---

> **Note:** Your class also uses `File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.*` — those are additional testability issues. Want me to generate wrappers for those too?
